WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.720
When I got into standard 10 years&nbsp;
ago, the question of what constitutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:09.200
a wincon was one with a single stand-out&nbsp;
answer—Aetherling. It was a control meta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.040
no doubt—if you weren’t playing a devotion&nbsp;
deck or attempting in vain to play green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.560
you were playing one of the many variants of&nbsp;
control, and you were almost certainly running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.880
one or two copies of Aetherling as a way to&nbsp;
close out the game. For a relatively new player,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:27.000
it was a comforting type of card—you input a bunch&nbsp;
of mana and two or three turns, and in return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.760
it reduces your opponent’s life total to&nbsp;
zero. So that’s what a wincon is, right?

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.520
Or maybe a wincon is when my brother played&nbsp;
an Overwhelming Stampede into a cluttered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.160
boardstate to cut through my modest suite of&nbsp;
blockers like a hot knife through, I dunno,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.280
a handful of 4/4 vampires. Or maybe it was&nbsp;
a wincon when I played Angelic Gift on a 4/4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.000
land at the Battle for Zendikar prerelease&nbsp;
and smacked my opponent four times for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.240
win. Above average two mana enchantment,&nbsp;
honestly, some might even call it a wincon.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.440
We all come across the concept of a win condition&nbsp;
somewhere along the line in our existences as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:05.360
magic players. And this is for a reason—MTG’s&nbsp;
defense-friendly combat system and hand-emptying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.000
mana system means that it can’t always be assumed&nbsp;
that the game will end naturally, that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.200
all sorts of game situations that might sprawl out&nbsp;
and demand some sort of remedy. There are lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.160
situations where the phrase “win condition”&nbsp;
has a fairly understandable meaning. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:26.040
as with most pieces of terminology, it becomes&nbsp;
a lot more challenging in Commander. Sextupling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:30.360
the life total of your opponents makes a lot of&nbsp;
things challenging, to be honest. Not only does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:36.480
it take more to win in an EDH game, but the&nbsp;
concept of what winning means shifts wildly.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.160
Conventional EDH wisdom might have you gesturing&nbsp;
at various things as a wincon—Craterhoof Behemoth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.400
is a wincon, Torment of Hailfire is a wincon,&nbsp;
Purphoros, God of the Forge is a wincon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:53.160
Sanguine Bond is a wincon. And sure, this&nbsp;
classification does work on some level—there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.520
are lots of situations where these cards will&nbsp;
win you the game, and they each gesture at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.960
different plan for winning the game. But is this&nbsp;
a useful category of card? I don’t think it is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:09.520
Anything can win a game—if your opponents play no&nbsp;
cards, a 1/1 squirrel is a quality wincon. If your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.720
opponents are playing nothing but archers and&nbsp;
bears, a single Siege Dragon is a great wincon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.920
You shouldn’t ask the question “what cards in my&nbsp;
deck are the ones that win the game,” because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:26.680
important question is “what cards in my deck are&nbsp;
the ones that don’t win the game?” Now, let me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:31.280
clarify that last statement—what I don’t mean to&nbsp;
say is that you should cram your deck full of big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:37.120
splashy bombs—quite the opposite. Having too high&nbsp;
a density of splash in your deck will leave you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.360
too little room for the building blocks that&nbsp;
actually lead to victory. What I mean here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:47.200
that, if you’re building a deck with the intent&nbsp;
of winning a game of commander, that goal should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.680
be present in some form or other throughout&nbsp;
each and every one of your card choices.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.240
The number one criticism I have for people&nbsp;
when they ask me to look at their deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.240 --> 00:03:00.920
a criticism I give about half the time, is&nbsp;
“this looks unfocused.” This phrase can mean a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:05.320
couple different things in practice, and to start&nbsp;
thinking about those, it’s useful to first lay out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:12.280
a framework for how EDH decks work. Fundamentally,&nbsp;
most decks involve a system of inputs and outputs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:16.960
with inputs being conditions that need to be&nbsp;
met and outputs being results of those inputs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.640
that contribute to the deck’s gameplan. My Sharuum&nbsp;
deck’s primary inputs are Astral Slide and cycling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.040
cards, and its primary outputs are flickers,&nbsp;
which allow Sharuum to recur large artifacts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:33.120
onto the board. My Meria deck’s primary inputs&nbsp;
are equipments, and its primary outputs are mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.800
value, and my commander hitting your face&nbsp;
for 14 damage. I could give more examples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.600
but long story short, inputs and outputs&nbsp;
are the name of the game for decks with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.280
any amount of synergy, and this framework itself&nbsp;
points us to the two places a deck can stumble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:51.480
with regard to focus—it can have unfocused&nbsp;
inputs, and it can have unfocused outputs.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.720
For an example of a deck with unfocused inputs,&nbsp;
a viewer emailed me about their Missy deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.800
contained both artifact creature and&nbsp;
facedown creature synergy packages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:04.920
with a couple narrower sections of sub-synergies&nbsp;
within these. Within the deck there are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.800
components to make some decently powerful&nbsp;
boardstates, but the issue is that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.840
are too many different types of card and ability&nbsp;
present, and they don’t really connect with each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:17.640
other outside of all being related to the&nbsp;
commander in some form or other. Ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:22.720
a deck like this with unfocused inputs will&nbsp;
naturally have lots of dead draws, and will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.160
struggle to put together a cohesive boardstate&nbsp;
with any consistency. It’s possible to build a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:33.120
deck that can pivot between a few different plans,&nbsp;
but these decks are tricky to build, and typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.440
involve paying close attention to points of&nbsp;
connectivity between various types of card.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:42.440
Some examples of decks with unfocused&nbsp;
outputs would be conventionally built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:47.640
variants of what I like to call value-tribal&nbsp;
commanders, and I’ll use Gavi, Nest Warden as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:52.360
an example since I’ve already talked about her&nbsp;
before. Your average Gavi deck is pretty good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:56.360
at maintaining cycling-based value loops,&nbsp;
but when we look at the actual payoffs for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:01.960
cycling aside from draw, it’s a lot of small&nbsp;
token creation, some larger token creation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:06.320
some soft creature removal, some incremental&nbsp;
damage, some creatures getting bigger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:11.760
and so on. Theoretically you’re getting a lot&nbsp;
of stuff when you add the input of cycling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.040
but those outputs are so diverse that these&nbsp;
decks don’t end up actually doing any one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:22.040
thing in particular—it’s just a flood of generic&nbsp;
Magic: the Gathering gameplay. This is probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:26.760
better than a deck with unfocused inputs because&nbsp;
it’s actually doing something pretty consistently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:30.800
but it’s a generally toothless type of deck&nbsp;
that will struggle to win against decks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:37.200
have more refined win plans, even if those decks&nbsp;
generate a smaller quantity of total resources.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.440
Both unfocused inputs and unfocused outputs&nbsp;
are problems that will lead to challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.480
in ending the game, but for different&nbsp;
reasons. Despite this, the solution to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:51.440
the two problems is pretty similar—you should&nbsp;
be asking what each card in your deck does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.120
how often it will fail to perform its intended&nbsp;
function, and whether it contributes toward a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:01.200
cohesive plan for your deck. A common first&nbsp;
step in building commander decks is to lump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.960
together a bunch of cards that synergize around a&nbsp;
particular one or two things, and while this will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.400
lead to a deck that does do something, if you’re&nbsp;
trying to win games it might take a bit of tuning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.520
beyond that to make sure that a deck can actually&nbsp;
make it to the finish line. And for a bit, I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:21.400
to zoom in on a particular type of deck that&nbsp;
often falls just a little short in this regard.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.560
Alright, this is a transition point in a&nbsp;
longer video where it might make sense to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.480
put a midroll ad, but I don’t like midroll ads&nbsp;
so I don’t run them on my videos. That said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:32.160
if you like what I do and have a spare&nbsp;
three bucks a month to support me in making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.400
high effort videos like this one, there’s a&nbsp;
link to my patreon down in the description.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:40.520
As I was going through the EDHRec page for&nbsp;
Gavi while writing the previous section,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:44.120
there was one standout exception to the&nbsp;
lack of good options for closing out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:50.000
game—that being Moonshaker Cavalry. The&nbsp;
card is a brutally efficient finisher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.760
capable of converting a board full of tokens&nbsp;
into a bunch of dead opponents in short order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:00.680
and its presence here gestures at a specific type&nbsp;
of card, mass buff cards, whose usage could lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:05.960
to a Gavi deck with a more definite identity.&nbsp;
This brings me to the subject of midrange—which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:11.040
I’ll define here as decks that want to get a big&nbsp;
board of creatures and then attack with them. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:15.880
I’m sure when playtesting we all like to imagine&nbsp;
the ideal scenario for a beatdown deck—we play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:20.040
out a bunch of creatures, and swing big into&nbsp;
the empty boards of our quivering hypothetical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:24.680
opponents. And this might occasionally be&nbsp;
the case, but in most games, your opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:29.600
will have creatures as well, even if their&nbsp;
deck isn’t a purely midrange deck. So here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:35.560
I’m going to introduce an important phrase—parity.&nbsp;
Parity basically means a state of standstill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:41.200
and in the midrange context it’s usually when&nbsp;
multiple players have decent-sized boards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:46.200
but can’t swing in for large damage due to a&nbsp;
lack of evasion or a risk of counterattack.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:50.360
In a game like this with multiple midrange-y&nbsp;
decks, we can divide the game into two key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:56.160
steps for a given player—“create parity”&nbsp;
and “break parity”. Creating parity just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:01.880
means getting your beefy midrange board up and&nbsp;
going. This sounds simple, and indeed it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:06.960
too hard in the abstract, but there are lots of&nbsp;
places to go wrong. You can fail to draw mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.320
fail to draw beaters, fail to draw&nbsp;
the right combinations of beaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.960
and even if you succeed at these three things you&nbsp;
can still get board wiped or lose to a non-combat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:20.720
wincon before you’re able to do an adequate&nbsp;
quantity of beating. For this reason, lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:25.640
ramp, draw, protection, and a varied mixture&nbsp;
of creatures might all be parts of how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:30.160
get into a state of parity with other decks.&nbsp;
Breaking parity is where you do something to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.560
the board that allows you to deviate from the&nbsp;
state of parity, which generally means letting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.120
your creatures hit for a lot of damage. The&nbsp;
most popular parity breakers in commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:45.480
are overrun effects, but there’s a myriad of&nbsp;
potential other options—targeted buffs, goad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:50.680
extra combat spells, mass evasion spells, and&nbsp;
cards that disable potential blockers, or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:57.080
remove them altogether. I am on the record saying&nbsp;
that Mass Calcify is the best evasion spell.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:01.680
Now, if I were to rank these in terms&nbsp;
of raw power, the bigger overrun effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:06.720
like Craterhoof Behemoth and Finale of&nbsp;
Devastation would be easily top of the pile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:12.880
with Moonshaker Cavalry being a similarly powerful&nbsp;
option in white. However, I’d argue that these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.280
also probably the least interesting options&nbsp;
as well given how straightforward and hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:21.200
interact with they are, so if you’re not&nbsp;
playing at a super high power level you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:26.040
should go with whatever parity breaker feels&nbsp;
most appropriate to you. The important thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:30.520
is that you have at least something for this,&nbsp;
some number of cards played with the goal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:35.840
converting that cool board that you worked hard&nbsp;
to build up, into a win. With all of that said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:41.240
and to crib shamelessly from EDHRECast, one&nbsp;
challenge in navigating parity breakers is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:46.680
being able to distinguish a card that helps&nbsp;
you win from a win-more card. The goal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:52.120
a parity breaker should be to turn a game state&nbsp;
that’s not quite winning into one that wins, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:57.280
some cards will only win with a boardstate that&nbsp;
was probably already about to win. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:02.440
Epic Struggle wins the game outright if you have&nbsp;
20 or more creatures, but there are a ton of cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:06.600
that can win off of a board that massive, and&nbsp;
most will be useful in situations with fewer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:12.440
creatures as well rather than being a four mana&nbsp;
enchantment with no text. Crucible of Fire is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:17.600
less extreme example of this—it’s an anthem for a&nbsp;
creature type that’s generally already quite big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:22.200
and most dragon decks would probably be better&nbsp;
served by running an additional dragon with useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:27.760
abilities instead. Ultimately, the way to navigate&nbsp;
these sorts of cards is to just think about all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:33.760
the situations in which a given card is useful,&nbsp;
and also the situations where it’s not useful. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:39.960
something is a kinda dead card two thirds of the&nbsp;
time, that other third had better be really good.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:45.400
Parity also exists as a relevant concept outside&nbsp;
of midrange decks, specifically for aggro and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:50.560
control decks. For an aggro deck, parity is&nbsp;
what you want to avoid—and if your momentum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:54.600
does come to a bit of a stall as your opponents&nbsp;
catch up on board, you at least want them to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.600 --> 00:11:00.360
low enough on life or other resources to be deeply&nbsp;
uncomfortable in their position within the game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:05.200
This is the general conceit of a lot of 60 card&nbsp;
burn decks—throw creatures at your opponent’s face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:09.720
until you can’t anymore, then hope you got your&nbsp;
opponent low enough to finish them off with burn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:15.640
in time. Control, on the other hand, is similar&nbsp;
to the classical midrange parity plan in the sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:20.480
that it’s looking to create and then break it,&nbsp;
but is very different in that control decks will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:25.400
often create parity by using board wipes or stax&nbsp;
pieces that slow down the table, and then break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:30.440
parity using carefully picked out cards that are&nbsp;
less affected by these tools. Control decks are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:35.200
also typically chiefly worried about resource&nbsp;
management—compromises must be made between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:40.680
how scary the board is allowed to be and how many&nbsp;
control spells are kept in hand for contingencies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:45.200
For these reasons, control is sometimes easier&nbsp;
to finish off opponents with than midrange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:50.320
since small boards and suppressive methods mean&nbsp;
that less potent finishers can still do the job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:55.160
and that winning over the course of several turns&nbsp;
is acceptable. You do still need to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.160 --> 00:12:00.040
the deck’s win plan, though, and balancing that&nbsp;
with the deck’s controlling elements is naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:05.920
going to be part of the challenge. On that note,&nbsp;
let’s talk about removal in a more general sense.

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:09.680
Based on the amount of removal people&nbsp;
seem to run in casual EDH it seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:14.280
a lot of people might not be aware of this,&nbsp;
but if one of your opponents wins the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:20.120
your odds of winning that game actually drop&nbsp;
quite precipitously. You might have a bit of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:26.600
hard time pulling off that W. So, seeing as how&nbsp;
this is Snail’s Guide to Winning an EDH Game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:30.560
it is my duty to tell you that preventing your&nbsp;
opponents from winning the game is an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:36.520
concern that you shouldn’t take lightly. However,&nbsp;
despite my smartassery, I understand why people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:40.800
tend to run low removal counts. Let’s assume&nbsp;
for a moment that we’re thinking about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:46.280
archetypal removal spell—a spell that destroys a&nbsp;
spooky thing on our opponent’s board that may or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:51.520
may not lead to our demise if left unchecked.&nbsp;
For every removal spell you add to your deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:55.640
you’re increasing your ability to hinder your&nbsp;
opponents, but you’re also subtracting from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.640 --> 00:13:00.680
slots available to bolster your own gameplan.&nbsp;
For this reason, you can think of these removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:07.320
spells as being something that buys you time, at&nbsp;
a cost. As a side effect of this, faster decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:13.880
won’t need as many removal spells as slower decks,&nbsp;
and I’m going to demonstrate why that is visually.

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:18.160
Let’s think about the amount of stuff that&nbsp;
needs removing at a given moment. When I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:23.040
think of really spooky stuff that begins to&nbsp;
happen in a game, it generally starts up small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:28.280
where say 10% of the time there will be some&nbsp;
potentially game ending nonsense on a given turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:31.480
but things will pick up for the next few turns&nbsp;
until you’re at the point when there’s gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:36.800
be spooky stuff left and right. The timing and&nbsp;
what exactly comprises Potentially Game Ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:41.720
Nonsense will vary with power level, but the&nbsp;
basic concept stays fairly constant and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:47.120
can shift around this scale as needed. Let’s say&nbsp;
you’re playing at a somewhat middling power level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:51.600
where you’re gonna see power plays starting to&nbsp;
trickle out around turn 5, with things getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:57.080
really intense by turn 9 or so assuming the game&nbsp;
goes that long. If you’re playing a faster deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.080 --> 00:14:01.400
say one that’s looking to start knocking down&nbsp;
opponents around turn 6, there’s a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:04.920
stuff that you simply won’t need to deal with,&nbsp;
because your opponents won’t have time to fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:09.200
develop it out before you’re threatening&nbsp;
lethal. By contrast, if you’re playing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:14.280
slower deck that’s looking to develop a much more&nbsp;
all-encompassing threat a bit later in the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:17.400
there’s a good chance you’ll need to contend&nbsp;
with some threatening plays from your opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:22.440
before you’re able to pull off your win, since&nbsp;
player removal is not an option. You’ll still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:27.760
want some ways to slow down your opponents in a&nbsp;
faster deck, but you won’t need nearly as many.

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:32.280
Another consideration is that interaction isn’t&nbsp;
always just about stopping your opponents’ win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:37.560
plans—it can also remove obstacles to&nbsp;
your win plan. With a given Flame Slash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.920
you could be killing your opponent’s Volo,&nbsp;
Guide to Monsters to prevent them from doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:45.280
some dumb shit with it, but you could also be&nbsp;
deleting somebody’s Glissa so you can swing at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:49.560
them with your offensively large attacker or&nbsp;
getting rid of their Archon of Emeria so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:54.320
can have your ridiculous pop-off turn&nbsp;
unimpeded. This sort of flexibility is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.320 --> 00:15:00.160
also where counterspells really shine—you can&nbsp;
always counter that Jin-Gitaxias or Tergrid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:05.680
but you can also counter a board wipe or a removal&nbsp;
spell lobbed at your key creature. I personally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:11.040
really like counterspells and usually run at least&nbsp;
8 in my decks with blue, and I’ll run more if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:16.480
win plan is more fragile, such as one that is&nbsp;
slow and board-based or a combo without backup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:21.920
options. Counterspells do have their downsides&nbsp;
though—there’s the holding-up-mana problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:26.200
where if you hold up mana for a potential&nbsp;
threat and that threat doesn’t materialize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:31.000
you’ve effectively wasted mana, as opposed&nbsp;
to a removal spell where you can wait for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:36.160
threat to materialize and then deal with it at&nbsp;
your next convenience. Counterspells are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:41.480
fantastic and can deal with some higher-powered&nbsp;
threats that are untouchable by normal removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:47.080
but they require a bit more thought to fit into a&nbsp;
game plan, and might not be right for all decks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:52.080
Regardless, it’s worthwhile to put in the thought,&nbsp;
and consider what sorts of interaction will best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:58.040
bolster your win plan, and whether it might be&nbsp;
worth it to play a bit more of a given deck.

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:02.040
I think it bears mentioning that everything I’ve&nbsp;
said so far in this video is meant in the context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:06.440
of a game where decks are similarly matched in&nbsp;
terms of power level, and where players are on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:11.440
the same page about what constitutes a fun game&nbsp;
of Magic: the Gathering. The most foolproof way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:16.440
to win a game of EDH is to obliterate a&nbsp;
bunch of precons with a Godo cEDH deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:20.960
so I guess if that’s what you clicked on the&nbsp;
video for, there you go, but I’d say that sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:26.240
of gameplay is not interesting for anybody.&nbsp;
In addition, for any given card that might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:30.800
be considered a finisher, there will be plenty&nbsp;
of players who aren’t interested in using it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:36.040
and others who would rather not play against it,&nbsp;
and navigating that is a key step in having fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:41.560
while playing a casual, social format. While&nbsp;
this video is about how to win an EDH game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:46.840
my target audience here is less so decks that are&nbsp;
already well-rounded and looking to pump their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:51.280
power level and more so decks that are doing&nbsp;
some cool things, but are still struggling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:56.360
find their way to wins. There’s a good chance&nbsp;
that improving your deck’s ability to win will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.360 --> 00:17:01.240
make it feel more powerful overall, but my point&nbsp;
here is simply that you should achieve that by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.240 --> 00:17:06.200
playing to your deck’s strengths and improving&nbsp;
its internal cohesion rather than just windmill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:10.960
slamming Craterhoof Behemoth or Torment&nbsp;
of Hailfire into it and calling it a day.

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:16.280
I also didn’t really discuss combo in this video&nbsp;
because I feel like combos are often conceptually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:21.800
more straightforward than other types of win&nbsp;
options since, though you can build a multivalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:26.880
combo family that can navigate around 5 different&nbsp;
types of stax effect, on the most basic level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:31.600
combo decks tend to be more of a yes-or-no&nbsp;
proposition when it comes to winning compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:37.720
a conventional aggro, midrange, or control deck.&nbsp;
I also made a full video about combo awhile back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:42.520
and I’ll briefly summarize a couple points from&nbsp;
that video here. Putting a combo in your deck can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:47.880
be a good way to win a game, but you should accept&nbsp;
that if you throw a combo in your midrange deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:52.840
if that combo is a lot better than your midrange&nbsp;
win plan, you’re not playing a midrange deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:58.600
you’re playing a weird, inconsistent combo deck.&nbsp;
Below higher powers, the best type of combo to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:03.840
in your deck will be one that requires some degree&nbsp;
of setup, but setup that fits naturally into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:09.560
rest of your deck’s plan, and whose pieces have&nbsp;
alternative use cases in your deck. Combo is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:15.400
basically synergy taken to the extreme, so combos&nbsp;
should ideally emerge from trying to optimize your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:20.880
synergies, not from throwing Sanguine Bond&nbsp;
and Exquisite Blood in a random black deck.

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:25.600
To recap some general pointers from earlier,&nbsp;
making your deck do a better job of winning will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:31.440
often just mean making sure the inputs and outputs&nbsp;
are focused. Keeping your inputs focused typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:36.080
just means having enough deck cohesion that your&nbsp;
deck can launch into a game plan off of its first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:42.640
10 or 15 cards it draws. You can think of this&nbsp;
as having a wide onramp—you want as many cards as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:48.240
possible that feel good early to give your deck&nbsp;
the best chances of getting into gear. Keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:53.195
your outputs focused means that once your deck&nbsp;
gets up and going, the things it does are ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.195 --> 00:18:58.000
that all contribute to a cohesive gameplan that’s&nbsp;
moving in the direction of victory. If you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:03.000
making a deck that does a lot of a particular&nbsp;
thing, such as discarding cards or sacrificing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:07.680
artifacts, it’s tempting to just throw in all&nbsp;
sorts of cards that have fun effects when those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:14.280
things happen, but doing that will just add filler&nbsp;
that doesn’t help you win. Wincons don’t exist in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:18.960
the sense that you’re gonna have a bad time if you&nbsp;
smash together a deck and then throw in a handful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:23.440
of splashy cards at the end for the purpose of&nbsp;
winning, but put a bit of thought in from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:28.240
start of where your deck wants to be going, and&nbsp;
you’ll have a pretty good chance of getting there.

